{
  "position": "We should start a scoped, measurement-driven effort this year that (1) profiles and refactors the worst bottlenecks in the existing ingestion pipeline and (2) designs and, if justified by the data, begins implementing a streaming-based replacement with a new storage format, recognizing that the storage change is a one-way door and must be treated as a deliberate migration rather than a quick fix.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "text": "Without profiling and benchmarking the current ingestion pipeline, we cannot credibly estimate the potential speedup from refactoring alone; instead, we should commit first to a short, focused measurement phase to identify whether the dominant bottlenecks are implementation-level (e.g., I/O configuration, serialization, single-threaded sections) or inherent to the current batch architecture and storage format."
    },
    {
      "n": 2,
      "text": "Both the current batch/storage design and any prospective streaming/storage design have one-way-door characteristics and long-lived switching costs, so we should explicitly weigh the migration and compatibility risks of a new storage format against the accumulating maintenance, scalability, and opportunity costs of keeping the current format, rather than treating only the rebuild path as risky."
    },
    {
      "n": 3,
      "text": "A three-year-old pipeline that is 'increasingly slow' is on a trajectory toward SLA violations and lost flexibility, so even if current daily or hourly SLAs are met, we should treat this as a near-term risk and evaluate both refactoring and streaming options for their ability to improve overall capacity (throughput) and resource utilization, not just latency."
    },
    {
      "n": 4,
      "text": "Streaming architectures can, in many workloads, improve both latency and effective throughput by enabling finer-grained parallelism, reducing redundant batch recomputation, and smoothing resource usage, so we should not dismiss a streaming rebuild purely on the assumption that it only addresses latency; instead, the decision should depend on whether our measured bottlenecks are best alleviated by local refactors or by a different processing model."
    },
    {
      "n": 5,
      "text": "Given the pipeline’s age and trend of slowing down, we should plan this year for a staged approach: improve observability and perform targeted refactors for quick wins while in parallel defining clear SLOs and drafting a migration plan toward a streaming architecture and/or new storage format that can support anticipated future features and load, with explicit checkpoints where we reassess whether to proceed with the full rebuild."
    },
    {
      "n": 6,
      "text": "The amount of 'headroom' a refactor can buy is highly uncertain ex ante and depends on empirical findings from profiling and load testing, so we should avoid assuming that an in-place refactor will reliably yield 1–2 years of breathing room and instead make continuation on the refactor-only path contingent on demonstrated gains against target SLOs."
    },
    {
      "n": 7,
      "text": "The decision about observability should not be biased toward either path: improving instrumentation of the current system helps both refactoring and understanding current load characteristics, while early design simulations, prototypes, and metrics for a streaming architecture help validate whether it would address the observed problems; both forms of measurement should inform the rebuild vs. refactor choice."
    }
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "Current SLAs are still being met or only narrowly missed, but trend data show growing runtimes, operational load, or fragility that will likely cause more serious issues within the next 6–18 months if unaddressed.",
    "The organization is open to a medium-term migration plan for storage and ingestion if justified by clear performance, reliability, or product wins, but prefers to avoid unnecessary disruption.",
    "Engineering capacity is constrained, so any streaming rebuild and storage-format migration would need to be staged with clear milestones and the ability to pause or redirect if early signals are unfavorable.",
    "We have or can quickly add enough observability (timers, metrics, tracing, profiling) to identify major hotspots and failure modes in the current pipeline within a few weeks of work.",
    "Downstream consumers of the current storage format can be enumerated and analyzed for migration impact, enabling a realistic assessment of switching costs versus the costs of preserving the status quo."
  ],
  "would_change_my_mind": [
    "Profiling results showing that the main bottlenecks are trivially addressable within the current architecture (e.g., misconfigured parallelism, easily shardable hotspots) and that modest refactors yield large, demonstrable performance gains with minimal complexity, making a full rebuild unnecessary in the next few years.",
    "Evidence that a streaming architecture plus a new storage format would not materially improve either latency or throughput for our workloads (e.g., dominated by a third-party API rate limit) or would introduce unacceptable reliability risks (e.g., extreme operational complexity beyond team capacity).",
    "Conversely, strong measurement and incident data indicating that the batch model and existing storage format inherently cause large recomputation, severe write amplification, or frequent SLA violations that cannot be resolved without an architectural shift and storage redesign.",
    "A clear business roadmap that either deprioritizes real-time/low-latency use cases for the foreseeable future (tilting toward deeper refactor-in-place) or, alternatively, makes such use cases central and time-critical (tilting strongly toward a streaming rebuild and storage migration).",
    "Organizational constraints or capabilities changing, such as the arrival of a platform team offering managed streaming infrastructure and migration tooling, or a major reduction in available engineering capacity that forces a narrower, refactor-only focus."
  ],
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "nuance": "Instead of treating refactor vs. rebuild as a binary, the most robust course is to use a short, intensive measurement and design phase to inform a staged plan that both harvests near-term refactor gains and prepares, if justified, for a streaming-based rebuild and storage migration, with explicit checkpoints based on observed performance, reliability, and product needs.",
  "changed_position": true,
  "because": [
    {
      "critic": "A",
      "claim_n": 1
    },
    {
      "critic": "A",
      "claim_n": 3
    },
    {
      "critic": "A",
      "claim_n": 5
    },
    {
